  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 206 PM MDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nA major pattern shift gets underway across the Intermountain \nWest today. Satellite imagery shows increasing cloudiness over \neastern Washington and Oregon associated with an approaching \nupper-level trough. Temperatures today have climbed quickly into\nthe 70s and 80s, marking the final day of 80-degree warmth for \nthe foreseeable future. Saturday represents the leading edge of \nthe cooler, wetter air mass, with lower-elevation maximum \ntemperatures struggling to reach 70 degrees. Showers and \nembedded thunderstorms will develop during the day on Saturday, \nelevating localized rainfall rates and initiating a multi-day \nprecipitation event.\n\nSunday will be the wettest and most impactful day of the \nforecast period as the upper-level low tracks slowly across the \nGreat Basin. A Flood Watch has been issued for most of eastern \nIdaho, extending from Sun Valley eastward across the Magic \nValley, Snake Plain, and the Eastern and Southeastern Highlands.\nWidespread rainfall totals of 1.0 to 2.0 inches are very likely\nbetween Saturday and Monday. National Blend of Models (NBM) \nprobabilities for storm totals exceeding 1.0 inch reach 80% to \n100% along and east of the I-15 corridor and into the South \nHills, with 2.0-inch probabilities ranging from 30% to 80%. The \nhighest QPF footprint is focused across the Eastern Highlands \nand toward Island Park, where localized 3.0-inch totals are \npossible. While persistent light-to-moderate rain is the primary\ndriver, embedded Saturday/Sunday convection could boost \nlocalized totals and create localized flooding concerns. Strong \ncold advection on Sunday will hold daytime highs in the 40s and \n50s.\n\nAs the primary upper low slowly crosses the Intermountain West,\nprecipitation chances will linger through Monday and Tuesday.\nRainfall intensity will wane compared to Sunday, with conditions\ngradually drying out by mid-week. Unseasonably cool air will\nremain locked over the region, holding daytime highs in the 50s\nthrough much of next week, with a slight recovery toward the\nlower 60s possible by late week. With cold air established\nbehind the departing system, attention shifts to overnight\nfreeze risks. Frost and freeze headlines may be needed as early\nas Sunday morning, with a much higher likelihood Monday morning\nthrough mid-week as skies clear during overnight hours. These\nchilly nights will likely result in the seasonal suspension of\nFrost/Freeze headlines for eastern Idaho at some point mid-week.",
